    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.a033275f327597e4fa80",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "To simplify syntax and to allow for dynamic components, such as date range filters, you can add macros to your query.",
      "headingPath": [
        "Microsoft SQL Server query editor",
        "Macros"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/datasources/mssql/query-editor/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "bbb58af24aced7414605130f50521c1a8bc6ca8c",
        "sha256": "9f485e1051e44b457c0808272de96a8616d75e95d5fb6d630bec71d225529f99",
        "lineStart": 115,
        "lineEnd": 115,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/datasources/mssql/query-editor/index.md#L115-L115"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "cloud",
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.7866d3ee720640c27535",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "Use macros in the `SELECT` clause to simplify the creation of time series queries. From the **Data operations** drop-down, choose a macro such as `$\\_\\_timeGroup` or `$\\_\\_timeGroupAlias`. Then, select a time column from the **Column** drop-down and a time interval from the **Interval** drop-down. This generates a time-series query based on your selected time grouping.",
      "headingPath": [
        "Microsoft SQL Server query editor",
        "Macros"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/datasources/mssql/query-editor/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "bbb58af24aced7414605130f50521c1a8bc6ca8c",
        "sha256": "9f485e1051e44b457c0808272de96a8616d75e95d5fb6d630bec71d225529f99",
        "lineStart": 117,
        "lineEnd": 118,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/datasources/mssql/query-editor/index.md#L117-L118"
      },
      "applicability": {
        "status": "oss-labelled-source-candidate-not-yet-reviewed",
        "products": [
          "cloud",
          "enterprise",
          "oss"
        ]
      },
      "atomicity": "unreviewed-may-require-splitting-or-deduplication",
      "acceptanceContract": null,
      "verificationStatus": "not-tested"
    },
    {
      "id": "grafana.candidate.8ce84a563bd35d0a6504",
      "syntaxRole": "declarative-or-imperative-paragraph",
      "sourceText": "Time macros (`$__time`, `$__timeFilter`, etc.) don't support time zone parameters in Microsoft SQL Server and always expand to UTC values. If your timestamps aren't stored in UTC (common with `datetime`/`datetime2` types), convert them to UTC in your SQL query using `AT TIME ZONE … AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'` rather than passing a time zone argument to a macro.",
      "headingPath": [
        "Microsoft SQL Server query editor",
        "Macros"
      ],
      "source": {
        "path": "docs/sources/datasources/mssql/query-editor/index.md",
        "gitBlobSha": "bbb58af24aced7414605130f50521c1a8bc6ca8c",
        "sha256": "9f485e1051e44b457c0808272de96a8616d75e95d5fb6d630bec71d225529f99",
        "lineStart": 121,
        "lineEnd": 121,
        "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/6193dc03311b631b9727b560d24369e683dc396e/docs/sources/datasources/mssql/query-editor/index.md#L121-L121"
      },
      "applicability": {
